title: A link's words are all a reader has to decide whether to follow it, so a link titled with a topic's name sends nobody, and one titled with a claim does.
pid: 8910393c-e7f6-4f29-9d64-6cd3a26eba85
body: |2-
  Information foraging theory (Pirolli and Card, PARC, 1999; see
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_foraging): a reader guesses
  what lies behind a link from its cues, which the theory calls its
  "scent", and leaves a place once what remains there is worth less
  than moving on. Where the scent is weak, the reader follows nothing,
  or follows at random. The team writes titles as claims ("A title is
  a sentence that makes the note's claim", in the note on how to write
  in the graph that every writer here contains); this is the same rule
  seen from the reader's side. Content is drawn whole and a link costs
  a request, so a link is crossed only if its words say what is behind it.
contains:
- 6be7c044-be23-4e2b-827b-1e7916b333c1
- 1a9c16a8-174f-492e-869f-1e1403cfe21f
